Subject: [PATCH] init: match boot options against __setup entries inline

Every boot option is compared with every __setup entry twice: once for
early params, once in obsolete_checksetup(). Both went through an out-of-
line parameqn() call per entry (per character, in obsolete_checksetup),
and with a return thunk those calls are most of the cost: 14 options and
273 entries make thousands of them.

Compare the characters inline, treating '-' and '_' alike as parameqn()
does, and only call parameqn() for early params once the first character
matches.

--- a/init/main.c	2026-11-04 11:04:09.352762062 +0100
+++ b/init/main.c	2026-11-04 11:04:09.372728179 +0100
@@ -196,6 +196,15 @@
 static const char *panic_later, *panic_param;
 
 /*
+ * Boot options are matched against every __setup entry, so compare inline
+ * rather than call parameqn() per entry: '-' and '_' are equal, as there.
+ */
+static __always_inline bool __init setup_char_eq(char a, char b)
+{
+	return (a == '-' ? '_' : a) == (b == '-' ? '_' : b);
+}
+
+/*
  * parameqn(line, str, strlen(str)), but stopping at the first mismatch
  * instead of measuring every __setup string for every boot option.
  */
@@ -204,7 +213,7 @@
 	size_t n;
 
 	for (n = 0; str[n]; n++)
-		if (!parameqn(line + n, str + n, 1))
+		if (!setup_char_eq(line[n], str[n]))
 			return false;
 	*len = n;
 	return true;
@@ -742,7 +751,8 @@
 	const size_t len = strlen(param) + 1;
 
 	for (p = __setup_start; p < __setup_end; p++) {
-		if (p->early && parameqn(param, p->str, len)) {
+		if (p->early && setup_char_eq(param[0], p->str[0]) &&
+		    parameqn(param, p->str, len)) {
 			if (p->setup_func(val) != 0)
 				pr_warn("Malformed early option '%s'\n", param);
 		}
